state.case_summary:
A man in his late 60s presented with a 4-week history of a rapidly growing left caruncular lesion. Medical and ocular histories were unremarkable.

Ocular examination findings were normal except for an amelanotic, pedunculated mass with an ulcerated surface, arising from the caruncle. There was no evidence of PAM (primary acquired melanosis).

{'Histopathology': {'Gross Examination': 'The specimen measured 8 × 6 × 5 mm and had a 4-mm ulceration with necrotic tissue on its surface.', 'Microscopic Examination': 'Revealed a solid tumor with an inflamed crust on its surface, composed of large anaplastic cells with mitotic activity.', 'Immunohistochemistry': 'Showed positive reactivity to melanocytic markers HMB45, MITF2, and Melan-A. The Ki-67 proliferation marker showed a significant number of cycling cells.'}, 'Images': {'Figure A': 'Ulcerated, pedunculated, caruncular tumor', 'Figure B': 'Proteinaceous material on the ulcerated tumor surface (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×25)', 'Figure C': 'Mitotically active tumor containing anaplastic epithelioid cells (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×400)', 'Figure D': 'Melanoma cells showing immunoreactivity for Melan-A (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×100)', 'Figure E': 'Nuclear staining for Ki-67 reveals a high proliferative index (hematoxylin-eosin, original magnification ×50)'}, 'Additional Tests': 'Sentinel lymph node biopsy findings were negative. Conjunctival and orbital biopsies revealed no tumor.'}
